\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces What each action does to the flows. Each panel is one junction seen top-down, the arterial running left to right with the cross street joining from above. In every panel the left of the stop line is the state at the decision instant (\textit  {now}: hollow glyphs) and the right is the same stream after the action (\textit  {after}: filled glyphs); the light pair at the top right is the \textit  {current aspect}, main then cross, so the reader can see the light the action is taken on together with the flow it changes. (a) With no intervention the bus waits out the red and the queue behind it grows. (b) \texttt  {GE} widens the arterial's green window by $\delta $, so the bus clears without stopping. (c) \texttt  {GR} truncates the cross-street green by $\delta $, bringing the bus green forward. (d) \texttt  {INS} adds a bus phase to the ring, so the bus is served this cycle instead of the next. (e) \texttt  {ER} ends the bus phase once the bus has cleared, handing green to the cross street early. (f) \texttt  {VP} drops an empty phase from the ring, removing two lost reds per cycle. (g) \texttt  {PT} re-sequences the ring so the bus phase is served first. (h) Offset correction shifts the upstream green band by $\Delta $ so the bus arrives on green. Panels are schematic and carry no measured values; \cref  {fig:action_mix} shows which of these the controller actually chose at each junction along the corridor.}}{7}{figure.caption.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:action_mech}{{1}{7}{What each action does to the flows. Each panel is one junction seen top-down, the arterial running left to right with the cross street joining from above. In every panel the left of the stop line is the state at the decision instant (\textit {now}: hollow glyphs) and the right is the same stream after the action (\textit {after}: filled glyphs); the light pair at the top right is the \textit {current aspect}, main then cross, so the reader can see the light the action is taken on together with the flow it changes. (a) With no intervention the bus waits out the red and the queue behind it grows. (b) \texttt {GE} widens the arterial's green window by $\delta $, so the bus clears without stopping. (c) \texttt {GR} truncates the cross-street green by $\delta $, bringing the bus green forward. (d) \texttt {INS} adds a bus phase to the ring, so the bus is served this cycle instead of the next. (e) \texttt {ER} ends the bus phase once the bus has cleared, handing green to the cross street early. (f) \texttt {VP} drops an empty phase from the ring, removing two lost reds per cycle. (g) \texttt {PT} re-sequences the ring so the bus phase is served first. (h) Offset correction shifts the upstream green band by $\Delta $ so the bus arrives on green. Panels are schematic and carry no measured values; \cref {fig:action_mix} shows which of these the controller actually chose at each junction along the corridor}{figure.caption.2}{}}
\newlabel{fig:action_mech@cref}{{[figure][1][]1}{[1][7][]7}}
\citation{Long2022PhaseSkipping}
\citation{Long2022PhaseSkipping}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces How TSP actions change from intersection to intersection (top-down, schematic). Each junction's dominant action family is shown, with the action count and Aimsun ID beneath. The mix is heterogeneous along the corridor: reallocation and phase rotation cluster where cross-street queues dominate, insertions where through buses are held, skips and early reds where phases are empty -- the same action set, differently deployed junction by junction. Counts are illustrative of one run, not a comparative result.}}{8}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:action_mix}{{2}{8}{How TSP actions change from intersection to intersection (top-down, schematic). Each junction's dominant action family is shown, with the action count and Aimsun ID beneath. The mix is heterogeneous along the corridor: reallocation and phase rotation cluster where cross-street queues dominate, insertions where through buses are held, skips and early reds where phases are empty -- the same action set, differently deployed junction by junction. Counts are illustrative of one run, not a comparative result}{figure.caption.3}{}}
\newlabel{fig:action_mix@cref}{{[figure][2][]2}{[1][7][]8}}
