\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Champion-search arms as actually scored. ``Actual reward'' distinguishes propose (family-internal ranking) from commit (final decision rule, Fig.~\ref {fig:objectives} Types~A/B/C/D/E). Type~A commits through the shared $Z_1$ layer $\arg \min \Delta _{\text  {net}}$ of \cref  {eq:net_pick}; Types~B/C/D/E commit on fairness/stability/rules/learned-Q and never enter that layer. All TSP arms ran with global-reward mode, measured state/queue feed, measured side cost, projected-saturation cascade cost, and $Z_4$ constraint mode; all were scored on eval seeds 300--700 at demand $\times $1.0 (learners pre-trained on seeds 800--1100, then frozen).}}{46}{table.caption.82}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:methods}{{9}{46}{Champion-search arms as actually scored. ``Actual reward'' distinguishes propose (family-internal ranking) from commit (final decision rule, Fig.~\ref {fig:objectives} Types~A/B/C/D/E). Type~A commits through the shared $Z_1$ layer $\arg \min \Delta _{\text {net}}$ of \cref {eq:net_pick}; Types~B/C/D/E commit on fairness/stability/rules/learned-Q and never enter that layer. All TSP arms ran with global-reward mode, measured state/queue feed, measured side cost, projected-saturation cascade cost, and $Z_4$ constraint mode; all were scored on eval seeds 300--700 at demand $\times $1.0 (learners pre-trained on seeds 800--1100, then frozen)}{table.caption.82}{}}
